# NOETHERVIM: BUNDLES

NoetherVim starts with no bundles enabled, and that is not an incomplete
install. The core edits any file well: language servers, completion,
treesitter, formatting, git signs, pickers and the file browser are all
there before you turn anything on.

A bundle deepens **one subject**. LaTeX, debugging, a git interface,
Obsidian notes, a test runner. You add the ones you actually work in.

Table of contents:

- [The catalogue](*catalogue*)
- [Turning one on](*enable*)
- [What lazy.nvim does with it](*lazy*)
- [Bundles that need each other](*pairs*)
- [Changing how a bundle is configured](*override*)
- [When something does not load](*trouble*)
- [Recap](*recap*)

## THE CATALOGUE *catalogue*

Press `<Space>cb`, or run:

~~~ cmd
    :NoetherVim bundles
~~~

Each row is a bundle: its name, what it provides, and whether it is already
active. The text is searchable, so typing `git` or `latex` narrows the list.

The categories say what kind of thing each is:

    `languages/`    a language worked in seriously: latex, rust, go, python,
                  java, c-cpp, web-dev
    `tools/`        debug, test, git, repl, database, http, ai, task-runner,
                  refactoring, octo, nvim-dev
    `navigation/`   harpoon, flash, projects, yanky, editing-extras
    `writing/`      markdown, obsidian, neorg, zotero, wrapsearch,
                  presentation
    `terminal/`     better-term, tmux, remote-dev
    `ui/`           colorscheme, eye-candy, minimap, helpview, tableaux
    `practice/`     training, hardtime

Each bundle's own file documents what it installs, what it binds, and what
it needs on your system. `<CR>` in the picker opens it, which is the honest
answer to "what will this actually do".

## TURNING ONE ON *enable*

In the picker:

    `<C-y>`   enable: uncomments its import line in your init.lua
    `<C-x>`   disable: comments that line back out
    `<CR>`    open the bundle's source, unchanged
    `<C-o>`   seed a file for overriding how it is configured
    `<F1>`    list every key the picker binds

`<C-y>` shows a diff of the exact change before writing anything, the same
prompt the templates picker uses. If your init.lua has been restructured
past recognition, it copies the import line to the clipboard and says so
rather than guessing.

Pick one you actually want and enable it. If nothing obvious appeals,
`tools/git` is a good first one.

Then **restart Neovim**. lazy.nvim installs the plugins on the next start;
they do not appear in the running session.

Fill in the key that enables the bundle under the cursor:

In the bundle picker, enable with ___

## WHAT LAZY.NVIM DOES WITH IT *lazy*

Enabling a bundle adds one `import` line to your init.lua. lazy.nvim reads
it, downloads what is missing, and pins the versions in a lock file.

Almost nothing loads at startup. A bundle declares when each of its plugins
is needed, and lazy waits for that moment: a filetype being opened, a
command being run, a key being pressed. This is why enabling several
bundles does not slow the editor down.

`<C-w>ll` opens lazy's own window: what is installed, what loaded, and how
long each took. `<C-w>ls` syncs, `<C-w>li` installs.

A bundle that needs an external tool says so, and NoetherVim fetches what
it can through Mason. Enabling `languages/rust` alongside `tools/debug`
gets you the Rust debug adapter, because the pair implies it. To manage
toolchains yourself, see [declining auto-install](noethervim-auto-install).

## BUNDLES THAT NEED EACH OTHER *pairs*

Two bundles are deliberately empty on their own, and this surprises people.

`tools/test` installs the test runner and **no adapters**. `tools/debug`
installs the debugger and almost no adapters. An adapter arrives from the
language bundle, and only when both are enabled:

    `tools/test` plus `languages/python`   pytest and unittest
    `tools/test` plus `languages/go`       go test
    `tools/debug` plus `languages/rust`    the Rust debug adapter

So `tools/test` alone gives you a panel with nothing to run, which reads as
a broken install and is not one. Enable the language bundle too. See
[test adapters](noethervim-test-adapters).

## CHANGING HOW A BUNDLE IS CONFIGURED *override*

Press `<C-o>` on a bundle in the picker, or `<Leader>e` from its source
file. A file is seeded in `lua/user/plugins/` listing the repository strings
that bundle configures, as commented stubs.

One rule decides whether your change works:

    `opts`     merges into what the bundle already set
    `config`   replaces it entirely

Reach for `opts` unless you mean to throw the existing configuration away.
An override that silently does nothing is almost always this distinction.

One more trap: merging does not extend lists. A key whose value is an array,
like a list of formatters or servers to install, is **replaced** rather than
appended to. To add to one, use the function form of `opts` and insert into
the table you are handed. See
[array-valued options](noethervim-user-plugins-arrays).

## WHEN SOMETHING DOES NOT LOAD *trouble*

If a bundle was renamed or removed upstream, lazy reports that no specs were
found for it. NoetherVim surfaces this as a notification after startup
rather than a message lost on the command line, and the notification carries
the key that opens your init.lua.

`:checkhealth noethervim` lists the same thing under spec errors, names the
current path when a bundle was renamed rather than deleted, and flags an
override left behind with nothing to extend.

